speechtext:
I am very sorry to have to disagree with my hon. friend from Elgin regarding the urgency of the case. There seems to be a general impression that this is not an important matter, that it is not a matter of urgency, and that because we have not had such legislation in the past, we do not need it in the future. No man can travel through Ontario in any direction without seeing evidence of the necessity of something being done to get rid of weeds. I am satisfied that this province loses hundreds of thousands of dollars per year because of me prevalence of noxious weeds. I do not think that there is any question coming before the parliament that is more important than the question of weeds in the farmers' grain.

III the North-west, in the district in which I live, I can assure the committee the farmers have suffered damage to the amount of thousands and thousands of dollars by reason of buying grain which contained the seeds of weeds. Now, it is true the country will not burn up if we do not pass legislation on the subject this year. It is also true that the legislation we may pass may not be effective for the purpose designed. But I do submit that we have to make a start some time if we are ever going to reach an end. Apparently, these weeds will not kill themselves ; apparently people will not cease trading in impure seeds, unless legislation and administration is made effective against such conduct. Well, if we are going to commence some time what better time is there than now and how can we commence better than by affirming the principle of legislation against noxious weeds by passing this Bill at this session ? If there is any question as to the details of the Bill, as to the effect its provisions will have if put in force ; if there is any danger, in the opinion of any hon. gentleman, as to delaying the business of parliament, all that that hon. gentleman need do is to agree to the amendment proposed by the minister which will prevent the Bill from being effective for another year, and will give everybody warning that parliament is in favour of the principle of this legislation, and that all must be prepared to protect their own interest by the time parliament meets again. But if the Bill is set over for another session, the country will take it for granted- and I think will justly take it for granted- that parliament is not in favour of the principle of the Bill ; for they will say that if parliament was in favour of the principle it would have adopted it. but that having refused to adopt it it is not in favour of it; and everybody dealing in impure seed will rest easy. And so, next session we shall find ourselves exactly where we are this session, with the House not prepared and the people not informed. I say, in the interest of the farming community, the greatest in the country, it is most important that the principle of legislation against impure seed should be adopted at this session by the passing of this Bill with the amendment proposed by the minister bringing the Bill in force next year.
Mr. HEYl). If we carry out the wishes of the minister, we shall start next year where we leave off this year, but, if we leave this an open question for another year, everything that has been said during the past week in connection with the Bill will have to be talked over again. If we pass the Bill as the minister wishes, farmers ard dealers will have a live question before them, though it will be some time before the measure becomes operative. If we do not cary out his wishes, we must start again where we are now and all our labours will be lost.	;
